  "surprise": "Talking heads (pre-softmax (8, 8) head-logit mixing per block, identity init, 4,800,384 params as registered) on 115-fable scored 561/519/517 on seeds 0/1/2 vs 115's 639/646/609: exact means 532.33 vs 631.33, -99.0, every seed down (paired -78/-127/-92), far outside the registered -45..+45 range (central +8). Discard. The mechanism was never tested at parity: the registered systems prediction (10-25% fewer steps) was wrong by a factor of 3. Leaving SDPA for explicit q.k logits, an einsum mix, a masked fp32 softmax and att@v materialises several (B, H, T, S) tensors per block, and pretraining ran 1419/1412/1419 steps vs 4204/4218/4263 (-66%; 211 ms/step vs 71 ms). The wall-clock cosine still finished, so val loss landed near 115 (0.0839/0.0810/0.0855 vs 0.0826/0.0806/0.0792) but the fit did not: held-out greedy 0.960/0.953/0.965 vs 0.9815/0.983/0.9845 and 27-33% round-1 parse failures. The RL start was worse on every seed (round-1 acc 0.234/0.196/0.219 vs 0.318, targets 665/617/655 vs 718/756/691) and the gap never closed (r4 1006/972/993 vs 1131 on seed 0). RL sampling speed was unchanged (255-300 samples/s vs 273-296; the cached single-query path is cheap) but fine-tuning on full sequences paid the same 3x, so rounds took 348-392 s vs 238-242 s and totals 2006-2041 s vs 1556-1572 (no timeout). Lesson: at this budget any attention change that cannot use a fused SDPA kernel loses on step count before its inductive bias can matter; a talking-heads test needs the mix folded into a fused path (or bf16 softmax with fewer passes) and a matched-steps control.",
